#let ink-soft = rgb("#6a6155")

= The Motion of Small Particles Suspended in a Liquid

#text(size: 8.5pt, fill: ink-soft, tracking: 0.12em)[
  ANNALEN DER PHYSIK · SUBMITTED 11 MAY 1905
]
#v(0.5em)

If the kinetic theory of heat @boltzmann1896 is to be taken literally, then a
particle small enough to be jostled unequally from its two sides must wander
visibly under the microscope, driven by the molecular motions themselves. The
wandering is a true random walk: it is not the distance but the _square_ of
the distance that grows with the time of observation. Along one axis,

$ angle.l x^2 angle.r = 2 D t $

where the diffusion coefficient follows from the molecular theory as

$ D = (R T) / N_A dot 1 / (6 pi eta a) $

for grains of radius $a$ in a liquid of viscosity $eta$ at temperature $T$.
Every quantity on the right is measurable except $N_A$ --- so the formula,
read backwards, _counts the molecules_.

#v(0.5em)
#text(size: 8.5pt, style: "italic", fill: ink-soft)[
  NB. --- A table of measured displacements belongs here; it waits on cleaner
